<?xml version="1.0"?>

<!-- vision60_perception's lidar with two numbers changed: 10 Hz -> 5 Hz and 75 m -> 5 m. The scan
     geometry is upstream's, and deliberately so. Everything else here, including the links and
     the IMU, is verbatim.

     Why not something smaller. Rays are free in this sim, so shrinking the scan buys nothing and
     costs detections. G4 on 2026-08-05, same gate, same machine, three dogs; repeats of one
     configuration came out 0.33 vs 0.40 and 0.70 vs 0.86, so read nothing into a gap under 0.15:

       world                                       lidar            RTF
       1 ms  (legged_gazebo/worlds/empty.sdf)      no               0.80
       1 ms                                        1024x32 @10Hz    0.37
       1 ms                                        512x8   @ 5Hz    0.40, 0.33   <- 16x fewer rays
       1 ms                                        512x8   @ 1Hz    0.53
       2 ms  (legged_admm_fleet/worlds/empty.sdf)  no               1.00
       2 ms                                        512x8   @ 5Hz    0.70, 0.86
       2 ms                                        1024x32 @ 5Hz    0.67   <- what this file sets

     Cutting the ray budget 16x moved nothing; only the rate dial did, and only a little. Nor did
     the GPU: forcing NVIDIA EGL with headless rendering gave 0.67 against 0.70 on llvmpipe, see
     the comment in worlds/empty.sdf. The cost is the per-update work around the render.

     And the narrow scan actively hurt, for 0.03 RTF. 512x8 over +-10 deg was sized for dogs
     sharing one plane, which they do standing still. Measured while they walked (the earlier
     check had run them collapsed with no controllers, which is not the same test): robot2 saw
     ONE of its two peers, 6 to 7 returns, at 1.48 m. They pitch and roll as they trot and a
     20 deg band slides off the peer. With upstream's geometry restored, same walking fleet,
     ground returns below z = -0.5 m dropped, three consecutive clouds:

       peer     returns   bearing      geometry   nearest
       robot1   20        29.5-29.6    30 deg     1.412 m
       robot3   22-37     91.1-91.3    90 deg     1.349 m

     Both peers, every sample, bearing within 1.3 deg. Nearest range reads under the 1.40 m
     centre distance because a lidar measures the near face of the body, not the centre, which
     is the honest quantity anyway (see the phys_gap_logger note in CLAUDE.md).

     Note 11264 of the 32768 returns are ground. Whatever consumes this topic has to drop them;
     the numbers above do it with a flat z threshold, which is enough on this arena's flat floor.

     What actually mattered was the physics step, and it was not a lidar question at all: this
     gate had been loading legged_gazebo's 1 ms world all along and never picked up the
     2026-07-30 move to 2 ms that arena_run gets through gazebo_package. Both are now on ours.

     So what is left is three changes from upstream, each with a reason:

       5 Hz          this is peer observation, not a control loop
       5 m range     obs_range is 4.0 m; nothing past it is evidence
       360 deg       upstream scans +-150 deg, leaving a 60 deg blind cone astern, and the
                     V formation puts the apex dog's two peers at exactly +-150 deg, on the
                     boundary. It sees a sliver of each, too little for the body-box fit to
                     work with, and it showed: with the same tracker and the same fit, the
                     two rear dogs reported p95 error 0.046-0.069 m while the apex reported
                     0.352 and 0.666. Rays are nearly free here (see the table above), a
                     spinning lidar is 360 deg in reality, and a structural blind spot in
                     the middle of a safety argument is not worth defending.

     So these values are for cleanliness and honesty about what the sensor needs to be, not for
     speed. -->

<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="lidar">
    <xacro:macro name="lidar" params="connected_to lidar_name xyz:='0 0 0' rpy:='0 0 0'">
        <joint name="${lidar_name}_joint" type="fixed">
            <origin xyz="${xyz}" rpy="${rpy}"/>
            <parent link="${connected_to}"/>
            <child link="${lidar_name}_link"/>
        </joint>

        <link name="${lidar_name}_link">
            <inertial>
                <origin xyz="0 0 0.03585" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <mass value="1.0"/>
                <inertia
                    ixx="1e-6" ixy="0" ixz="0"
                    iyy="1e-6" iyz="0"
                    izz="1e-6"/>
            </inertial>
            <visual name="visual">
                <origin xyz="0 0 0.036" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry>
                    <cylinder radius="0.052" length="0.072"/>
                </geometry>
                <material name="darkgray"/>
            </visual>
            <collision name="collision">
                <origin xyz="0 0 0.03585" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry>
                    <cylinder radius="0.052" length="0.072"/>
                </geometry>
            </collision>

            <visual name="mount_post">
                <origin xyz="0 0 -0.06" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry>
                    <cylinder radius="0.025" length="0.12"/>
                </geometry>
                <material name="darkgray"/>
            </visual>
            <collision name="mount_post_collision">
                <origin xyz="0 0 -0.06" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry>
                    <cylinder radius="0.025" length="0.12"/>
                </geometry>
            </collision>
        </link>

        <joint name="${lidar_name}_optical_joint" type="fixed">
            <origin xyz="0 0 0.036" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <parent link="${lidar_name}_link"/>
            <child link="${lidar_name}_optical_frame"/>
        </joint>

        <link name="${lidar_name}_optical_frame"/>

        <gazebo reference="${lidar_name}_optical_frame">
            <sensor name="lidar" type="gpu_lidar">
                <always_on>1</always_on>
                <update_rate>5</update_rate>
                <topic>$(arg sensor_topic_prefix)${lidar_name}</topic>
                <ray>
                    <scan>
                        <horizontal>
                            <samples>1024</samples>
                            <resolution>1</resolution>
                            <min_angle>-3.14159</min_angle>
                            <max_angle> 3.14159</max_angle>
                        </horizontal>
                        <vertical>
                            <samples>32</samples>
                            <resolution>1</resolution>
                            <!-- +-45.4 deg -->
                            <min_angle>-0.79238</min_angle>
                            <max_angle>0.79238</max_angle>
                        </vertical>
                    </scan>

                    <range>
                        <min>0.5</min>
                        <max>5.0</max>
                        <resolution>0.001</resolution>
                    </range>
                </ray>
            </sensor>
        </gazebo>

        <gazebo reference="${lidar_name}_optical_frame">
            <sensor name="${lidar_name}_imu" type="imu">
                <always_on>1</always_on>
                <update_rate>100</update_rate>
                <topic>$(arg sensor_topic_prefix)${lidar_name}/imu</topic>
            </sensor>
        </gazebo>

        <gazebo reference="${lidar_name}_joint">
            <disableFixedJointLumping>true</disableFixedJointLumping>
        </gazebo>
    </xacro:macro>
</robot>
